  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) achieved only 48.5% accuracy, suggesting the linear PCA representation may be a significant bottleneck. Question: Does a learned nonlinear RNA encoder (scvi_n200) substantially improve over PCA while holding all other factors constant (same model setting, profile setting, no image)? This is a clean single-variable A/B comparison that isolates the encoder effect. Why this is the best next experiment: With 8 experiments remaining, I need to determine whether the RNA representation is the primary limitation before investing budget in multimodal or multiprofile configurations. If scvi_n200 shows a large jump, it validates investing in richer encoders (scgpt) and justifies pairing them with image later. If it shows little change, it signals that RNA signal is inherently limited and I should pivot toward image-only or multimodal configs. This controlled comparison maximizes information per experiment under the tight budget."
}

